1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,296
How do you keep a card number so that even a stolen database is useless?

2
00:00:04,346 --> 00:00:09,816
In Lesson 5, the card went straight into the card zone, and only a token came back.

3
00:00:09,866 --> 00:00:15,816
In Lesson 6, Vault held every key, and no key was ever typed into a file.

4
00:00:15,866 --> 00:00:21,960
This lesson uses Vault to seal each card number, so even a stolen database is useless.

5
00:00:22,010 --> 00:00:23,832
Picture the worst day.

6
00:00:23,882 --> 00:00:25,872
A copy of the database leaks.

7
00:00:25,922 --> 00:00:29,184
If the card table holds numbers, every card is gone.

8
00:00:29,234 --> 00:00:34,056
If it holds numbers locked with a key that sits next to them, every card is still gone.

9
00:00:34,106 --> 00:00:36,600
So the key must never sit next to the data.

10
00:00:36,650 --> 00:00:39,384
Remember the locked room from earlier lessons.

11
00:00:39,434 --> 00:00:42,672
Inside it, every coat goes into a sealed bag.

12
00:00:42,722 --> 00:00:45,696
Only one machine can seal a bag or open it.

13
00:00:45,746 --> 00:00:48,000
That machine never lends out its key.

14
00:00:48,050 --> 00:00:50,568
In the card zone, that machine is Vault.

15
00:00:50,618 --> 00:00:54,792
The card vault, the service that keeps cards, hands Vault the card number.

16
00:00:54,842 --> 00:00:56,712
Vault hands back a sealed value.

17
00:00:56,762 --> 00:00:59,304
The card vault keeps only the sealed value.

18
00:00:59,354 --> 00:01:01,176
The key never leaves Vault.

19
00:01:01,226 --> 00:01:03,288
Each seal is tied to its own card.

20
00:01:03,338 --> 00:01:06,912
A sealed value copied into another card's place will not open.

21
00:01:06,962 --> 00:01:09,504
So nobody can shuffle bags to trick the room.

22
00:01:09,554 --> 00:01:11,376
Now a puzzle.

23
00:01:11,426 --> 00:01:14,808
Sometimes the card zone must find a card again by its number.

24
00:01:14,858 --> 00:01:17,568
It must do that without opening every bag.

25
00:01:17,618 --> 00:01:21,120
So Vault also stamps each bag with a fingerprint.

26
00:01:21,170 --> 00:01:24,312
The same number always gives the same fingerprint.

27
00:01:24,362 --> 00:01:27,192
The fingerprint cannot be turned back into the number.

28
00:01:27,242 --> 00:01:29,544
This stamp is called a blind index.

29
00:01:29,594 --> 00:01:32,592
The card zone looks up the fingerprint, never the number.

30
00:01:32,642 --> 00:01:34,752
The database guards the door too.

31
00:01:34,802 --> 00:01:37,728
It refuses any card value that is not sealed.

32
00:01:37,778 --> 00:01:41,232
Even a mistake in the code cannot write a plain number into it.

33
00:01:41,282 --> 00:01:43,104
What if Vault is down?

34
00:01:43,154 --> 00:01:44,976
Then the card vault stops.

35
00:01:45,026 --> 00:01:49,008
It never guesses, and it never falls back to keeping a plain number.

36
00:01:49,058 --> 00:01:53,640
Stopping safely instead of carrying on unsafely is called fail closed.

37
00:01:53,690 --> 00:01:58,824
A number is opened for one reason only: to hand it to the card's network for a charge.

38
00:01:58,874 --> 00:02:03,864
Every use of card data is written down: what happened, who asked, and when.

39
00:02:03,914 --> 00:02:07,680
And a card nobody has used for the bank's chosen time is removed.

40
00:02:07,730 --> 00:02:11,400
Only a short note keeps its token and its masked number.

41
00:02:11,450 --> 00:02:13,272
Now watch the product.

42
00:02:13,322 --> 00:02:17,064
There is no screen inside the locked room, so watch what each side sees.

43
00:02:17,114 --> 00:02:20,640
The buyer types a card into the card form and confirms the code.

44
00:02:20,690 --> 00:02:24,840
The form says "Card accepted" and empties its number field.

45
00:02:24,890 --> 00:02:31,128
The payment page then shows one line: the first six digits, a row of stars, and the last four.

46
00:02:31,178 --> 00:02:34,728
Beside them sit the card's network and the bank that issued it.

47
00:02:34,778 --> 00:02:38,952
That masked line is all anyone outside the room ever sees.

48
00:02:39,002 --> 00:02:40,824
Now Vault stops answering.

49
00:02:40,874 --> 00:02:44,832
The buyer types a card, and the form refuses it with a short message.

50
00:02:44,882 --> 00:02:47,928
Nothing is saved, sealed or unsealed.

51
00:02:47,978 --> 00:02:53,616
On the bank's side, the team gets an alert: a service the card zone depends on is unavailable.

52
00:02:53,666 --> 00:02:55,776
The alert names the part that failed.

53
00:02:55,826 --> 00:02:58,800
Once Vault is back, the same card goes through.

54
00:02:58,850 --> 00:03:00,672
Now the leaked copy again.

55
00:03:00,722 --> 00:03:02,544
An attacker opens it.

56
00:03:02,594 --> 00:03:06,408
They find sealed values, fingerprints and masked numbers.

57
00:03:06,458 --> 00:03:08,280
The keys are not there.

58
00:03:08,330 --> 00:03:15,072
A backup is just as useless without the Vault that sealed it, so the bank keeps both, and keeps them apart.

59
00:03:15,122 --> 00:03:16,944
What does this protect?

60
00:03:16,994 --> 00:03:21,312
It protects every cardholder whose number has ever passed through the room.

61
00:03:21,362 --> 00:03:26,616
A stolen database, a stolen backup, or a curious insider all see noise.

62
00:03:26,666 --> 00:03:31,728
And the bank is protected too, because a leak of noise is not a leak of cards.

63
00:03:31,778 --> 00:03:33,600
Back to the sealed bags.

64
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:35,472
A machine that keeps its key.

65
00:03:35,522 --> 00:03:38,256
A stamp that finds a bag without opening it.

66
00:03:38,306 --> 00:03:41,640
A database that refuses anything unsealed.

67
00:03:41,690 --> 00:03:44,928
So a stolen copy of the database is just noise.

68
00:03:44,978 --> 00:03:47,592
Next: what a card's first digits tell you.
